      "quotes": [
        {
          "text": "As part of the practice of Business Architecture, we separate the concern of what we do (business capability), from who does it (organization), from how value is achieved (value stream), from how it is implemented or performed at an operational level (process), from the information that is needed, from the systems that are used, and so on. Figure 1-2 shows the relationships between value, value streams, and value stream stages (discussed further in the next section), and other foundational Business Architecture concepts.",
          "source_block_ids": [
            "G178:p0012:b023",
            "G178:p0012:b024",
            "G178:p0012:b025",
            "G178:p0012:b026",
            "G178:p0012:b027",
            "G178:p0012:b028"
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "For example, Figure 1-2 shows that a Stakeholder receives Value, and that a Value Stream consists of one or more Value Stages (note that the direction of the arrows indicates the object of the relationship in a subject-verb-object grammatical structure).",
          "source_block_ids": [
            "G178:p0012:b029",
            "G178:p0012:b030",
            "G178:p0012:b031"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "selection": "caption-reference-and-nearby-context"
